    <div class=3D"o-article__intro">
    <div class=3D"f-deck"><p>For modern and contemporary artists working be=
tween Africa and the diaspora, film and photography have been tremendously =
important forms of creative expression. <br class=3D"softbreak"></p></div>
  </div>
 =20
  <div class=3D"o-article__body o-blocks">
    <p>The five moving-image makers featured in <em>Screens</em> share the =
planetary hopes of Pan-Africanism, a set of bold visions first developed be=
fore 1900 that have galvanized global struggles for freedom and solidarity =
ever since.</p><p>The films underscore the great attention paid by African =
and African diasporic contemporary artists to legacies of the 1950s and =E2=
=80=9960s. In those decades, people of African descent worldwide achieved u=
nprecedented gains in national sovereignty, cultural expression, and politi=
cal recognition. That heyday of decolonization and civil rights sharpened P=
an-African imaginations: fostering visions of contemporary self-affirmation=
, on the one hand, and on the other, a global connectedness for Black peopl=
e. <br class=3D"softbreak"></p>

<figure data-type=3D"image" data-title=3D"&lt;p&gt;Still from &lt;em&gt;The=
 Meaning of Various Photographs to Tyrand Needham, &lt;/em&gt;2009=E2=80=93=
10&lt;br class=3D&quot;softbreak&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;" class=3D"m-media m-me=
dia--s m-media--contain o-blocks__block">
